WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900
Jairus, 300 billion people are counting on you, so don't choke!

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:28.540
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.440
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.360
I'm Sean.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.240
I'm Joe. And I'm Sam.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:45.920
I don't know, I feel like there's got to be more than 300 billion people in a fully functioning galaxy that has, you know, that has, like, figured out time, not time travel, but space travel at the least.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.800
Yeah, but you only ever see our solar system, in which case you assume it's only the Earthlings, right?

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.320
300 billion Earthlings, okay.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.040
Yeah, that's a lot of Earthlings, though.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.680
There could be, like, a couple of things going on, like, Mars at this point, but beyond that, I don't know if we could get any more.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.740
Yeah. It's weird.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.400
It's a weird number.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.300
Did it say people, or did it say, like, life forms?

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.940
People. Oh, okay.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:16.380
And it is AD 2500, so, um, is that how you would say that year, if we were living in that year?

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.240
It just feels wrong. I think it'd be 2500.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:25.520
2500, right. Because we were living in the year 2000, so I thought, like, maybe you would say 2500, but I like the year 2500 better.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.740
I feel like if you were, like, in the year 1900, you'd say 1900, right?

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.560
That's what we used to say.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:33.900
That's also true. That's what we used to say.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.740
We never said we were in the year 1900.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.760
Of our lord. We should have.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.520
Only of our lord. This is legendary NES shmup developer Konami.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.100
Don't be fooled by that ultra game stuff.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.580
This was developed by Konami.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:57.540
And, uh, the reason why I say legendary NES shmup developers specifically because a lot of their games have a great chance of being on our essential games list.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.480
Uh, Lifeforce, Gradius, uh, even Stinger was on there for a minute.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.800
So, uh, sky's the limit, or maybe solar system's the limit in this game, uh, for what they're capable of.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.520
Yeah. And I did say Gyrus at the start of this episode.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.260
We kind of joked at the end of last episode, how do you say this?

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.520
I went on the ArcadeMuseum.com to find out how to pronounce Gyrus.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.780
And, basically, people were saying, well, it's not Greek.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.720
So you wouldn't say it like Yyrus.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.540
That's what I was saying.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.180
That's enough proof for me. That's all that they could, is that all that they could, uh, rule out?

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:33.380
Just that it's not Greek?

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.760
I think eventually the conclusion was Gyrus, but I guess there's no reason why it can't be Gyrus.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:46.140
Yeah. I mean, if it means anything, whether or not there was actually, like, a real way to say it, I'd still say Gyrus.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.400
That's all I need to know.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.300
So, uh, two people can't be wrong.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.600
Uh, you know, that's the fail-safe system around here.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:03:16.120
Yeah. Anyway, it's, as I mentioned, AD 2500, and mankind has penetrated the galaxy's fringes, establishing colonies of peace from Mercury all the way to Pluto.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.900
Suddenly, the tranquility of the Milky Way erupts into a frothing fury as armadas of alien death ship swarms.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:25.780
Those are their words.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.580
I'm not coming up with anything playful here.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:35.200
These alien death ship swarms invade our solar system like killer bees preying on an innocent bystander.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.180
You'll notice that none of these alien ships in the game look like killer bees.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.880
So now the, like, the manual's really getting into the nitty gritty of it.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.820
See, I don't even see that in my manual.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.180
I think I'm reading off the arcade manual.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.680
I think that's right. Oh, okay.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.620
Yeah.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:56.380
Uh, they mention that no one is spared by these killer bee death ship swarms.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.820
Even beloved Mother Earth is stung.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:17.400
These vicious cosmic visigoths, uh, are war-starved gyrisons, uh, and, and they're led by the immortal Genghis Khan, whom they recruited from Mongolia centuries ago.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:18.960
Wow. That's incredible.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:30.460
So that is, okay, because in, in, in the other manual, it just says Genghis Khan, and I thought maybe, like, oh, there's a guy who took that name, uh, you know, out of inspiration in the future or something.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.560
They mention it just briefly that a picture of Genghis Khan appears, and that's all.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:36.600
Not, not just a picture, though.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.700
A neo-revolutionary portrait.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:46.260
Yeah. Of Genghis Khan. Yeah.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.840
Genghis Khan. And why it's neo-revolutionary, I'm not sure.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.020
But it's great.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.140
It also just, like, it, like, delivers that in the manual as, like, part of the instructions.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:01.340
Yeah. It's like, when the opening screen appears, displaying a neo-revolutionary portrait of the savage Genghis Khan, press start.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:02.720
Yeah, I love it.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:09.660
That's literally what it says. It's how to save the universe, and then no backstory, and it's just, when you see the picture of Genghis Khan, press start.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:19.020
I also like that they chose Genghis Khan as, like, the big reveal that, like, oh no, he didn't ever die or lose.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:31.180
Actually, he was recruited by, he was so good that he was recruited by these aliens centuries ago just to wait to strike once the, like, because Earth wasn't enough for him, he needed the entire solar system to conquer.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:38.920
Incredible. Also, shout out to the use of the word zaptifying, uh, describing how many stages there are.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.540
I don't know if that's a word, or even if it was a word, if it has any meaning, but it's great.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.400
I believe there's 39 zaptifying stages, right?

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.840
Absolutely. That's, that's correct.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:06:01.480
So, uh, with our galactic fleet flattened, you're the lone space cowboy, again, they're words, but I love that, uh, you're the lone space cowboy who escaped the gyrus shroud of doom.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.060
I'm in. I'm hooked.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:11.740
Yeah. Sometimes we're like, you know, there's not enough investment in the story or the plots of these games to, like, understand what's going on.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:20.180
I feel like this was the right level of absurdness meets, like, hell yeah attitude for me that I was like, I want to beat the game.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.880
Like, I want to take down Genghis Khan and reclaim the solar system.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:33.420
Yeah, I mean, now that you've given me, like, an extra piece of deep lore that we didn't get with the NES manual, I'm definitely down for it.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.960
Like, just what's in the NES manual, it feels like a drunken rambling.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:44.300
But when you give that context that's not in the actual manual, it definitely works better.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.280
Yeah, the manual has a little bit of attitude in the beginning.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:07:03.480
Like, when it's saying what the planets are, it says, uh, for those of you who flunked astronomy, those planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.440
Right? Which is good.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.460
Those are the planets.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:13.020
But you don't start on Earth or Pluto, you start on Neptune, or you start by approaching Neptune.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:23.940
You gotta figure that maybe you were based in Neptune, and then it was like, oh, right, right, Pluto is a planet now again in 2500, like, right, right, I gotta go back and save that too.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.380
And then you go the other way.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:42.660
Well, here's the thing, maybe what they're saying is that, uh, because the elliptical orbit of Pluto, uh, actually intersects the orbit of Neptune in 2500 AD, Pluto will be within Neptune's orbit.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:46.360
Can you explain everything just in that voice going forward?

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:59.200
Absolutely. All right, but before we get too deep into where you go in this game and what you do, I feel like we do need to explain that this isn't, uh, the shmups like Life Force and, uh, and Gradius and stuff.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:04.260
This is a little different, kind of like, uh, Tempest, but with some visual flair to it other than vectors.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.860
Joe, do you want to give a good stab at explaining what you do in this game?

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:10.940
Yeah, because at my first thought, I was like, oh, this is different.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.260
And then, like, you know, a couple seconds in, I was like, wait a minute, this is a shmup.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:19.620
Like, it tricked me at first, because it's, it looks like you're going forward, you know?

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:27.140
It looks like it's, it looks like it's like, um, I don't know, like a more modern, like, rails shooter that's, like, in 3D space.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:32.480
But what it really is, is kind of a shmup that's been, that's been rolled up into a cylinder.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:40.280
So instead of going side to side on the screen, you kind of roll around the outside of the screen as, as the stars come at you and all your enemies come at you.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.560
And it is actually kind of cool, it's a nice little change of pace.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:45.900
Um, but that gives you all kinds of different things to think about.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:52.000
Like, the fact that you can't shoot across the screen, because you're shooting into the center of the screen, which is, like, further in Z space.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:59.500
So, I thought that that was actually a nice little, uh, a nice little refreshing touch on, on shmups.

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:07.440
Yeah, I, I very much appreciated that they, they did something unique with the presentation.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:15.360
Um, and I guess all that you need to keep me from getting bored is to, uh, make it a circle.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.860
So, I also thought that it, it was a refreshing shmup.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:25.540
Exactly. Yeah, I, I think it really changes the way you think about your movement.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:32.780
And, like, zigzagging is a lot different, and it has different implications in this than it does in a typical shmup.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:37.140
And the fact that you also can't move forward and backward, I think is interesting, too.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:45.300
Yeah, so you're really, you're going around in a circle here, and I think we should talk about that decision a little more of being able to only control your ship in a circle.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.220
Because they do give you two different control choices, control A or control B.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:55.220
The difference being control A, you actually use all four, uh, D-pad buttons.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:02.500
So this way, as you make your way around the circle, you have to switch between using the left D-pad, the up button, the, the right.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:13.900
Uh, and then control B is only using left and right, and at times that can get tricky, too, because you might be at, like, um, you know, three, you might be at three o'clock.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:20.720
And now all of a sudden, left suddenly might feel different than going right if you were locked in that space for a second.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:24.140
You do have to kind of keep track of where you are.

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:27.300
Did you guys have a preference, control A, control B?

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:38.880
I'm actually surprised that they made control A, which is the more context-based, uh, control scheme, uh, that they made that, like, the first choice.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:55.820
Um, I guess, like, now, whenever, I don't, I can't even think of a game in which this is sort of, like, you're always going around in a circle, but, um, I would assume that it would be this control B, where you just move left and right, and depending on where you are on the screen,

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.660
that's going to move you, uh, clockwise or counterclockwise.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:08.280
I, I prefer control B, uh, because it's sort of got, like, it just has that one rule, clockwise or counterclockwise.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:17.320
Um, though I guess control A may make more sense to some people that, like, it's more of, like, an absolute, uh, direction.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.600
Um, but yeah, definitely control B for me.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.960
Yeah, I, I tried, um, control B first.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:36.980
And, and I liked it, but I, you know, it did get confusing when, uh, a little bit, until you get used to it, when, um, you know, right, I don't remember which one's which, but let's say right takes you clockwise and left takes you counterclockwise.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:40.320
Suddenly, when you're at the top of the screen, that feels reversed.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:42.940
It's not, but it feels, when you hit right, suddenly it feels like you're going left.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.440
Well, you are going left, but you're going to the ship's right.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:46.780
You know what I mean? Yeah.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:48.220
So that would get confusing once in a while.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.820
So I thought, okay, let me, let me start over and I'll try it with control scheme A.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.720
Um, because then I can just press the direction that I want to end up in.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:01.940
If I want to end up on the left side of the screen, no matter where I am on the screen, I can press left and it'll take me towards that side of the screen.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:17.040
Um, and I liked it for a minute, but I, I pretty quickly went back to the first control scheme scheme because I think the problem for me with control scheme A is that it's harder to be fluid, like to have fluid motion.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:23.660
Because if you're, if you press up, once you reach the uppermost point of the screen, you do an abrupt stop in any direction that you're going.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:33.060
So that kind of, uh, while, while the other, the other control scheme might have a little more of a learning curve, I think if you have much more of a handle over it with that.

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:53.120
Yeah. It's essentially the same like argument for like, if you're playing like an old school, uh, survival horror, not, not quite as old school as Friday the 13th, but like resident evil, um, where whether you're doing tank controls, which again, it's just clockwise or counterclockwise.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:13:16.480
Or if you're doing like absolute directionality of whatever, uh, uh, whatever camera angle you have, I think it's sort of, you run into the same problems with both where you can get turned around basically, uh, with the, uh, with tank controls, but it is, it's basically more, uh, consistent, at least in my opinion.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:19.080
I don't know if you guys see the same similarities there.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.180
You know, I think that's all, that all makes sense to me.

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:23.440
I was a control B guy as well.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:34.040
Uh, but I also think like this might not have been just the best decision for a control scheme with a D pad, you know, like, like for a D pad, it's suffices.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:43.240
I don't know what they even did at the arcade to be honest, but it feels like the actual correct way to play this game would be with a, like a wheel, right?

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:48.220
And just like spinning around in a wheel to understand the direction at all times.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:52.080
And like, that's something they could have done at the arcade, I suppose, but I don't think they did.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:53.560
That's kind of what I'm looking for.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:58.180
But I wonder, can you guys think of a better way to handle controlling yourself around the ship?

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.520
It would take an analog shit, an analog stick, I think.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:06.500
Uh, if it's just going to be D pad, I think it has to be one of these two situations.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:09.040
Yeah, I, I don't know.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:10.580
I was also a control B guy.

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:22.860
I tried to do control A for the, for a while, because I thought it was interesting that they have this kind of unique scheme, but sort of what everyone's been saying, it's a little bit too context dependent.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:27.480
Like if you're on this side of the screen, then up will send you up in this direction.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:30.920
But if you're on this side of the screen, up will send you up in the other direction.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:35.320
And it just got a little bit too confusing for muscle memory's sake for me.

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:41.180
So I went back to A and B, but I wasn't, uh, Arkanoid or something.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:43.520
The one that had a dial.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:44.980
It did have a dial.

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:46.360
Yes, it had a dial controller.

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:49.160
Yeah, I think that would be perfect for this game.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:50.700
I wonder if it would work.

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:52.260
It's an NES controller.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:53.900
I feel I would love to try that.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.100
Oh, so Arkanoid came with the controller?

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.380
It came, it came on the NES with a dial controller that you could plug in.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:02.540
And that's how you could play specific to Arkanoid.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:05.260
But I don't see why it wouldn't work if you plugged in.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:06.740
I mean, it only has one other button.

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:11.800
So you wouldn't be able to use your special power up, but you would be able to fire the blaster theoretically.

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:14.960
Interesting. But maybe you wouldn't even be able to start the game.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.420
I don't know. I don't have that controller.

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:24.640
Yeah, but I think if they had made it, if they had made it like that, but with a, another button, I guess.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:26.200
Yeah, totally.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:36.500
Another interesting thing about being in a circle, and I think Joe touched on this when he was explaining the game, is that the enemies, they fly in mostly from the outside.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:38.380
Some come directly from the center.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:45.940
But when they fly from the outside, as far as I could tell, they actually don't hurt you on their way in.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:53.000
Like, you can't accidentally be surprised by like a jump scare alien ship that then like hit you immediately.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.500
Like they get to fly into the center.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:16:01.020
And then as they get closer, as they work their way from the center back to you, that's when they're on the attack.

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:07.220
But where they place themselves in the center matters because you're shooting into deep space.

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:11.660
So you can't just be on like the left side and hit something on the right.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:14.860
You'll just shoot into the left side of deep space.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:23.940
You have to go over there and it's a little more precise than what we're used to usually with shmups that are either horizontal or vertical because those you just shoot across the screen.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:26.240
And if it ever makes contact, it makes contact.

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:28.040
This you're kind of shooting into a void.

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:34.040
So I like the enemies can't hit you right off the bat and just, you know, take free lives from you.

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:43.740
But there's also like this interesting challenge of not letting the enemies develop quick enough so that there's too many on a side or that things become overwhelming.

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:51.240
Like you kind of have to attack them while they're far out because the closer they get back to the outer circle, the tougher it's going to be for the player.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:17:00.660
Yeah, I think that if they were to make you like allowed to get hit as they're coming in, this that would have been a deal breaker to play this game at all.

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:03.380
But no, I do.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:08.160
I do like the idea of again, it's sort of like a crowd control thing.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:24.780
But I guess a lot of shmups are like that where you're just trying to make sure that they can't get back to you because there are a lot of enemy types that at least later in the game make it pretty easy for them to get right up in your grill.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:29.160
And that's sort of what you're trying to avoid the whole time.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:59.140
There's also this weird design decision, though, that like, and I don't know if it's because the game is an architecture.

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:01.280
Arcade game, so it's mostly based around points.

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:10.440
But if you're not on the same, you know, plane as those enemies, let's say you're at the bottom of the circle and they're approaching from the top,

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:16.900
they're just going to kind of continue to drift out into the corners of the screen until they are no more.

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:19.180
And there's no penalty for missing them.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.660
You just don't get those points.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:32.320
But theoretically, you could do like a pacifist run where you don't really shoot at anything and just wait till everything enters the center of the screen and then clears back out.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:37.340
And then you'd be able to move on to the next stage, which are referred to as warps in this game.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.540
I think that that's a little weird.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:41.480
Can you confirm that?

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:43.040
Have you tried?

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:45.380
Have you to play a pacifist run?

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.260
Or at least like for one level.

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:50.140
Yes, I didn't do a complete like I did.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:57.360
I did shoot things, but I definitely was able to let a significant amount of things go by and then and move on to the next level.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:18:59.000
They don't just continue to spawn.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:00.720
Gotcha. It's like I wouldn't.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:04.000
I wouldn't have known because I was I was shooting stuff.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:11.800
But I was wondering if it was like it keeps going until you clear or you kill this many enemies.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:13.480
But I guess no, it's just on a timer.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:17.280
So that's best I could tell. It's either a timer or a set pattern.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:22.620
You know, like once it's spawned the last one for that level and granted the levels do get a little bit longer.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.980
Once it's spawned that last one, that's like that's it.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:27.680
And you either get the points or you don't.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:34.080
What about the structures that come up in levels that are like mini bosses almost that those you have to take care of?

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:36.300
Yeah, right. You have they don't leave the center.

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:44.740
OK, I wondered about what you were saying to about like since other than those big structures that Sam just mentioned, can you know, could you just go through the whole game?

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:46.980
And like what's the penalty for not doing it?

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:53.460
And my thought on it is just that you probably could do it and you're probably going to it's probably going to be a harder game for doing that.

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:56.040
So it doesn't feel like you're cheating by doing that.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:02.140
Like the reason my incentive for shooting as much as I could was to get things out of my way, you know, and I let things go by when they weren't bothering me.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:06.660
But usually I wanted to kind of clear things off the screen so it was never congested, you know.

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:13.820
And plus, I think the more the more you kill, you get more ultra lasonic phaser boosters, which makes the bosses easier.

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:20.060
Right. And also, like, do you want to let Genghis Khan have that last laugh of like you didn't you didn't fight his fleet off?

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:27.000
And anybody you let go by is just going to go back to Pluto and clear out that house because you're not there anymore.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:34.740
Right. Yeah. Do you think that like if you let them go by, but then you wind up getting to Genghis Khan that like that's it?

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:35.680
Like they're just going to give up?

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:36.840
They're going to be like, ah, you know what?

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:38.000
We're done taking over Pluto.

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:40.360
Actually, this is this this war was for naught.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:42.380
Yeah, I think there's an alternate ending.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:49.600
Yeah, they just keep going out into deep space like they don't stop since we're talking about the game here.

00:20:49.600 --> 00:21:00.200
I feel like we missed the biggest thing, the eye opening moment for me, at least starting this game and hearing Takata and Fugue and D minor.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:08.820
Wow. That was impressive for NES sounds, but also just like really brought on the like, OK, like, let's do this.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:12.940
Like I am ready to take down this this dreadful fleet of enemies.

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:16.600
And then the rest of the music is pretty is pretty playful and exciting.

00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:18.620
And, you know, it's a fast boost of stuff.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:23.520
But I don't know. There was like an adrenaline kick to that like eight bit version of Takata and Fugue.

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:26.860
Yeah, no, I like the music.

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:28.040
I didn't recognize it.

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:30.020
I don't know how I didn't recognize it.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:32.740
But it is very energetic.

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:34.600
And Sam, you're telling me you felt nothing?

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:36.540
When that song came on?

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:39.540
When that song came on, you weren't like, whoa, that's that's awesome.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:40.880
I thought it sounds awesome.

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:41.980
I thought it was cool.

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:49.380
But like my first reaction whenever I hear a song that is not there, like whatever, whatever the game is not.

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:57.300
My first reaction when I hear a song that's not original and specific to the game is like, oh, they just picked a different song.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:02.040
They didn't come up with one on their own, you know, and even though shame Konami.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:04.420
I mean, a little bit.

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:06.400
That's just how I react to it.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:11.040
Because I feel like you have the chance to set the stage for this game.

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:12.440
It's your game.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:16.980
You did the art. You did the, you know, the gameplay, the coding, all of it.

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:18.840
Like, why would you use someone else's song in it?

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:20.640
It just that seems a little weird to me.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:27.340
I don't know. I mean, I kind of thought a little bit of that, especially honestly, particularly because it was such a recognizable song.

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:34.560
And it's just so like, it almost feels like a parody at this point because that's like in everything that's supposed to be like ominous.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:38.100
It was like that. It was maybe that choice that actually threw me off a little bit.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:43.860
But I'll say that I completely have, it doesn't bother me at all because of how good I think the rest of the music is in this game.

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:46.880
I think it's pretty good. Yeah, there's something about the music in the game.

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:54.520
Because it's so fast and fun, it complements the stages well, too, because we haven't talked about this.

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:57.540
But the stages are pretty short bursts of enemies.

00:22:57.540 --> 00:22:59.960
And you can clear through warps pretty fast.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:07.420
I'd say it's like maybe a minute, minute and a half of total gameplay for a warp stage before you move on to the next one.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:16.200
And that was like really refreshing that it wasn't like a five minute long stage and there's 39 of these and it's going to take forever.

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:19.060
And granted, you know, do the math, it is still a very long game.

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:26.040
But I liked how quick and energetic it was at the start to allow you to flow.

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.980
I think you can get from Neptune to Pluto relatively quickly.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:31.520
Yeah, I appreciated that.

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:42.980
And like the between planets, which basically are just the boss battles, they do sort of change up the...

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:49.600
I mean, the basic gameplay doesn't change from level to level, but at least they will throw different patterns at you.

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:55.240
So it's not like a minute of one thing and then another minute of the exact same thing.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.400
They do some things to spice it up.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:02.120
Between these like mini levels or warps, I guess.

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:14.720
But I guess they could have also just squished them all together and it would have just been like nine or ten, like five minute long levels.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:17.600
But I guess there's no reason to do one or the other.

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:20.920
But I did like the bite-sized nature of them.

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:38.420
I also think it was cool the way that they made a few levels between each planet, but still the planets being like recognizable landmarks were like a nice little bookmark for you, the player, to make you feel like you're making progress.

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:40.700
For sure. Yeah, that's what I was gonna say.

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:43.660
I think it's more about the progress in the decision to make it bite-sized.

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:46.680
I don't think that's like something that would work for every game.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:54.180
I think like Bionic Commando would be really weird if they were just like short one and a half minute levels and then you just kept moving across.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:55.380
You're like, I'm doing something.

00:24:55.380 --> 00:25:00.480
You know, in games like that, it kind of does help to pad out the levels to make them feel more epic.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:11.100
This, I don't know, something about like the way you clear out ships, the music, the speed at which you're moving at too around the circle to take care of the different enemies.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:14.240
It just felt like it worked really well for being bite-sized.

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:20.400
And also with the arcade nature of the game, just made everything feel like it was moving a lot quicker than it probably was.

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:23.140
Because 39 stages is an awful large amount of stages.

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:31.220
Yeah, and it's just nice to like, while they have like the different stages between the planets, it's just nice to not necessarily just be like,

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:33.760
all right, I'm on stage one and I'm about to be on stage two.

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:36.240
It's like, I'm at Neptune.

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:39.460
Like, I can contextualize that and it makes it feel a little cooler.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:41.980
Yeah, it feels more like a journey.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:52.480
Yeah. I also think it's like if you die and I don't know, in other games, going back to the beginning of a level is like,

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:55.000
a really weighty penalty.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:58.680
And I didn't really die that much in this game.

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:03.000
So I'm trying to think if that's the penalty for a restart or a game over.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:05.940
But just something...

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:07.280
You're asking when you get hit?

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:09.740
Not when you get hit, but when you run out of lives.

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:12.400
Then the whole game... Oh, I think when you run out of lives, I think that's game over.

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:15.320
That's game over. Okay, then I just...

00:26:15.320 --> 00:26:21.100
Never mind that. But I also enjoyed the bite-sized nature of it.

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:24.040
And it does feel like you're progressing really well.

00:26:25.100 --> 00:26:33.320
I also appreciated that in the beginning, you can kind of see a pattern of what the bosses are like,

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:38.360
where I think the first two are just, you know, blow up these little eyes.

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:45.460
And then you move on to another boss and it's just got more eyes that you have to blow up.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:48.560
And they more or less stuck to that.

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:52.220
But there were a few planets that had something new.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:57.100
Like, I think Earth, you fight three other spaceships that are kind of like you,

00:26:57.180 --> 00:26:58.120
but coming from the inside.

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:05.480
And I forgot what planet has a boss that's like an orb with arms.

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:08.140
Oh, yeah. That was...

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:14.500
I think that's either the end of Pluto or the end of Urinet.

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:23.360
Yeah. So I appreciate that they didn't just stick to the same skeleton for each boss,

00:27:23.380 --> 00:27:27.900
even though a lot of them are kind of the same, just with more bulbs.

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:34.460
Well, would you be shocked if I told you that boss battles were added specifically for the NES version of this game?

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:35.880
They didn't exist in the arcade.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:37.120
That I didn't know.

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:42.220
The arcade version didn't have boss battles and also didn't have the super blaster.

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:43.860
I don't know what you called it, Sean.

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:45.240
You had a very fancy name for it.

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:52.080
But that B button that does like a super powered attack that can kind of just kill enemies in one shot,

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:54.120
that didn't exist in the arcade either.

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:58.800
And I feel like that's very surprising that the NES got things that the arcade version didn't.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:04.740
So you're telling me that the arcade version only had the Neo-Teutonic cannon or the Neo-Bomblastic cannons?

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:07.420
There was no ultra-lazonic phaser booster?

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:10.760
There were no ultra-lazonic phaser boosters in...

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:12.480
Can you imagine? Yes.

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:18.160
I know, Joe, the nerve of the arcade version to just be like, you know what?

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:19.260
This is actually just a game.

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:20.820
Well, you know what? Because here's the thing.

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:23.860
We haven't talked about it, but this arcade game is old.

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:28.660
Like, the NES is getting this game in 89, but I think it came out in like 83, the arcade,

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:32.180
and was clearly doing like Tempest meets Galaga.

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:37.080
So, you know, they probably just didn't think that they needed much more than that,

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:38.960
considering what was successful at the time.

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:43.860
But I think it's just wild to think that the, you know, we're always ragging on NES games

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:47.000
for being like, well, you know, the arcade version, the graphics look better,

00:28:47.260 --> 00:28:50.200
and it has more going for it, and it's more fun.

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:52.400
I don't know. Here, I'm actually shocked.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:55.960
Like, the NES version is like the feature-complete version of this game.

00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:57.820
It's another...

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:01.960
The word of the day is refreshing that we're not playing the dregs.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.800
And we talked about saving the solar system,

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:38.720
as opposed to just some other made-up planets that the developers would make.

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:41.420
And, Sam, I think you said that felt like a journey.

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:46.540
I like that description, because I do think that saving our solar system

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:52.620
is something important in terms of investment at this early on in video games

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:53.900
that we haven't really seen, right?

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:56.460
Like, sometimes you get World War II games or whatever,

00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:59.280
and those are like, you know, relive history in video games.

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:04.340
But this is like sci-fi meets grounded...

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:08.260
Not reality, but groundedness in some kind of way, you know?

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:09.980
Yeah, I don't...

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:11.120
I mean, I guess so.

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:12.940
If you don't read the manual,

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:18.640
and especially if you don't have the extra appendix that you have,

00:30:18.860 --> 00:30:21.820
it just looks like you're shooting at aliens

00:30:21.820 --> 00:30:24.580
until at the very end you have to fight the sun.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:28.220
The sun has like a tummy ache or something,

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:31.640
and then you have to make it not hurt anymore,

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:33.180
and then at the end it just says,

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:34.800
there is peace in the universe.

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:38.940
So I don't really know how grounded fighting the sun is,

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:41.240
but I see what you're saying.

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:42.600
At least it's real places.

00:30:42.980 --> 00:30:45.420
I like the idea that the peace in the universe

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:46.620
was just like a coincidence.

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:49.480
Like, you get to this... You defeat the thing at the sun,

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:50.460
and it's like, oh, check it out!

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:54.480
There's peace in the universe! I also just like the fact that the ultimate goal of this

00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:55.640
is to save the sun.

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:00.060
Well, that's what was holding us back from peace, right?

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:01.320
It's like, we don't know it,

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:03.320
but we actually have an evil sun now.

00:31:03.500 --> 00:31:05.020
You know how like in Majora's Mask,

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:06.100
the moon is evil?

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:09.180
Like, the sun in our reality is actually evil,

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:10.300
and we just have to beat it

00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:11.700
in order to achieve world peace.

00:31:11.700 --> 00:31:13.540
Yeah, maybe it's like the Mario sun.

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:15.980
Oh, the angry sun.

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:17.500
Yeah, terrifying.

00:31:18.140 --> 00:31:19.500
But yeah, even in the manual though,

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.700
it says, ultimate victory will occur only after you free

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:24.820
the solar system's life-regenerating sun.

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:27.080
So it's basically saying that

00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:28.900
they're holding the sun hostage.

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:30.920
And I feel like it's got some...

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:34.120
What's that? Stockholm Syndrome.

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:36.060
It's like the sun fights you.

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:37.160
I don't appreciate that.

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:40.740
So there is a lack of power-ups in this game.

00:31:40.740 --> 00:31:42.120
That's usually what we talk about

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:43.260
when we talk about shmups.

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:45.880
I don't even know if the dual blaster thing

00:31:45.880 --> 00:31:47.420
even counts as a power-up.

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:48.720
You get it almost immediately,

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:50.360
and then it's like,

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:52.000
does it... Is it even more powerful?

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:54.480
Like what? Now it takes like half as many shots?

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:57.560
I doubt it. It felt like it was not doing anything at all.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:31:59.420
So what do you think?

00:31:59.540 --> 00:32:01.580
Like, should there have been power-ups

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:02.940
that make it so this way

00:32:02.940 --> 00:32:05.340
you can shoot from, you know, different...

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:06.700
Like, I don't even know what you would do

00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:08.220
because you're going on a cylinder here,

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:10.140
but like, could you shoot in a spread

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:12.840
or shoot from behind you

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:16.940
or a laser that could go across the center

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:19.080
and actually hit those things over on the other side?

00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:20.880
Would that just have made the game too easy?

00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:23.240
Did you not care the power-ups weren't on there?

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:25.740
Somebody saved me from talking too much?

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:28.280
I was just gonna say, I think that it could have been cool

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.580
to have more power-ups. I do think that they'd have to increase the difficulty,

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:32.140
but it'd be nice to see...

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:33.520
I don't even know what it is,

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:34.900
but once in a while you hit something

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:37.340
and like two little stars come out

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:38.940
and they spiral around the outside.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:41.680
You just said exactly two points

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:43.100
I was about to exactly say.

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:44.940
We should have just said them together.

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:49.540
We should have just said them in unison. Yeah, I think that this game is really

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:52.200
on the easier end of the shmups that we've played.

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:55.260
So it would definitely need a difficulty spike

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:57.520
to need power-up.

00:32:57.860 --> 00:32:59.460
And yeah, I also thought that

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:00.800
that was gonna do something.

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:02.620
I thought that that was gonna be like,

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:04.560
that's just a special way

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:06.400
to get stuff on the opposite side of the screen,

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:08.260
but I think it was just for flair.

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:11.240
Yeah. So it seems like a missed opportunity.

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:12.960
Oh, you're talking about... Wait, wait, wait, wait. You're talking about the thing

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:14.660
that goes around in the center,

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:16.680
those two dots, and then it connects with you?

00:33:16.820 --> 00:33:18.200
Yeah. Yeah, I think it's when you get a level up

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:20.500
or when you get a light. Those give you the super blaster.

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:21.560
Yeah, those give you

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:23.420
the dual neobomblastic cannons.

00:33:23.600 --> 00:33:25.080
Yes. But where do they come from?

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:27.040
The visual element of it

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:29.360
makes it look like it's supposed to hurt things.

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:31.400
Oh, yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:32.800
And it also just shows us

00:33:32.800 --> 00:33:34.300
that they can do that.

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:36.500
So why not make a bullet that does that?

00:33:37.260 --> 00:33:38.200
Yeah, exactly.

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:39.700
But to answer your question,

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:41.620
those come from the mines.

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:43.840
So you'll see these little sets of...

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:45.720
They look like jacks almost.

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:48.860
And there will sometimes be

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:50.780
a little object in the middle of them,

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:51.840
and if you hit the object

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:52.700
in the middle of them,

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:53.680
that's what releases

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:54.880
those two little things.

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:56.260
And that will give you

00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:57.480
the dual blasters.

00:33:58.460 --> 00:33:59.360
Oh, okay.

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:01.020
Yeah. Cool. See, I like that.

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:03.060
I like that context. I wish I had noticed that

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:04.980
because it kind of makes it a little...

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:06.360
It's nice that it wasn't just

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:07.740
pick up this power up.

00:34:08.040 --> 00:34:10.240
That kind of goes into...

00:34:10.240 --> 00:34:12.300
Or for me, that kind of goes into this

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:14.500
issue that I have with the game

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:17.880
a little bit, which is that because of the nature...

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:19.280
Because they want to show

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:21.140
that you're traveling forward

00:34:21.140 --> 00:34:23.600
through space, they have the background

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:24.720
kind of coming at you

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:27.440
a little bit. But that's also how

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:29.980
all the projectiles

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.720
travel at that same speed.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:33.420
And sometimes, for me,

00:34:33.480 --> 00:34:34.320
I found that I got

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:35.860
the projectiles a little bit

00:34:35.860 --> 00:34:37.420
confused with the objects

00:34:37.420 --> 00:34:40.400
in the background. So it just seems...

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:42.460
Yeah, I can see that. It seems a little busy.

00:34:42.640 --> 00:34:43.560
And so when those things

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:45.460
come around, it...

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:46.360
I don't know.

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:47.620
I think it'd be interesting

00:34:47.620 --> 00:34:49.560
to see more objects

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:50.360
behave that way,

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:51.520
whether it's things

00:34:51.520 --> 00:34:52.360
that fire at you

00:34:52.820 --> 00:34:54.060
or you have to

00:34:54.940 --> 00:34:56.520
outrun things on the outside

00:34:57.620 --> 00:34:58.420
that are...

00:34:59.680 --> 00:35:00.580
I think I said

00:35:00.580 --> 00:35:01.480
the opposite of what

00:35:01.480 --> 00:35:03.780
I meant to say. You firing those

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:05.320
at enemies

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:06.820
or having to outrun

00:35:07.540 --> 00:35:08.480
projectiles that the enemies

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:09.840
fire at you that behave that way

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:11.240
to a certain point.

00:35:11.660 --> 00:35:12.720
Yeah, it would have definitely changed that.

00:35:12.780 --> 00:35:14.860
And to your point about them looking confusing

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:15.680
because they look like

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:17.000
the stars that are

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:18.400
coming at you, I think one thing

00:35:18.400 --> 00:35:19.820
they could have done to make it a little easier

00:35:19.820 --> 00:35:21.040
is make all the stars

00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:22.800
one color. The stars are like

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:24.100
a multi... It looks like a Pop-Tart.

00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:25.340
It's just like a multicolored

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:27.940
thing of sprinkles

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:30.120
coming at you. So then when a projectile

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:31.320
comes at you, it kind of blends in

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:32.460
because no matter what color it is,

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:33.560
there are a couple stars

00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:34.740
around that are that same color

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:35.660
moving in the same direction

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:37.780
at the same speed. Well, you should be

00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:38.800
dodging the stars too.

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:40.380
Just get good. Oh, right.

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:47.180
Right, I should. So, 39 stages.

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:50.360
Chance stages, which are kind of like

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:51.200
no consequence,

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:52.500
shoot all the enemies

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:53.860
and get points.

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:55.720
Boss battles

00:35:57.040 --> 00:35:58.380
and traveling across

00:35:58.380 --> 00:35:59.580
the nine different planets

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:00.760
plus a boss fight

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:02.020
with the sun, as John mentioned.

00:36:02.460 --> 00:36:03.760
Is there enough here

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:06.180
for you guys to continue to play

00:36:06.180 --> 00:36:07.400
through the entire game

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:08.400
regardless of, you know,

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:09.720
whether you're good enough to get there?

00:36:10.040 --> 00:36:11.220
Did this game

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:12.240
with everything that it has

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:13.160
in it right now

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:14.440
hold your attention

00:36:14.440 --> 00:36:15.940
or were you hoping

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:17.540
for more as the game went on?

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:20.280
It's pretty low content

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:23.260
compared to what we've been playing recently.

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:24.420
I think this would fit

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:27.020
better in the early days,

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:29.080
like 86. This would sort of

00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:30.940
be more

00:36:32.080 --> 00:36:33.120
among its peers

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:34.500
in terms of like how much content

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:36.320
is in the game. We did get more

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:37.140
than what was

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:38.140
in the arcade version,

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:39.640
but I still think

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:41.080
that it's not quite

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:42.940
a full priced game

00:36:42.940 --> 00:36:44.780
if we're going by that.

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:46.280
Yeah, I completely agree

00:36:46.280 --> 00:36:47.140
like that.

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:48.460
I do think I would have

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:49.480
seen it differently

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:50.300
early on,

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:52.520
but also, I mean,

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:55.240
I've got to say I'm just not the guy

00:36:55.240 --> 00:36:56.740
to tell you.

00:36:56.900 --> 00:36:58.440
You know, like me personally,

00:36:58.660 --> 00:36:59.700
no, it didn't hold my attention,

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:01.100
but that doesn't mean I think it was bad

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:02.300
or wouldn't hold anyone's attention.

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:03.840
Probably someone who likes shmups

00:37:03.840 --> 00:37:05.200
would really enjoy it,

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:07.200
but that's why

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:08.160
I find these games

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:09.420
so much harder to judge

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:10.820
just because of my personal

00:37:10.820 --> 00:37:12.300
opinion on shmups,

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:15.520
you know, is not particularly

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:16.780
positive, which doesn't mean

00:37:16.780 --> 00:37:17.680
that I think that the game

00:37:18.260 --> 00:37:19.520
is bad for everyone.

00:37:19.680 --> 00:37:21.400
It's just not my cup of tea.

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:22.800
I think that the

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:24.440
gameplay loop

00:37:24.440 --> 00:37:25.700
is good, though,

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:27.160
and even though

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:28.120
there's not a lot

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:30.280
of variation, the main thing

00:37:30.280 --> 00:37:31.500
that you do in this game

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:32.680
feels good

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:33.720
to do

00:37:33.720 --> 00:37:35.060
over and over,

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:36.560
and the fact that it's in these

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:37.840
little bite-sized bits

00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:39.400
helps a lot,

00:37:39.440 --> 00:37:40.420
and I think that helps

00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:41.300
make you want to

00:37:41.300 --> 00:37:42.460
go more because you know,

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:43.460
oh, it's just this much

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:45.300
until I get there, and this much until I get

00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:47.840
to the bonus stage, or chance stage,

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:49.320
and a chance to

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:50.320
increase my weapons

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:51.360
if, let's say,

00:37:51.400 --> 00:37:52.360
I just died or something.

00:37:54.380 --> 00:37:56.520
And, I don't know,

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:57.840
I feel like there

00:37:57.840 --> 00:37:59.020
isn't a lot in it,

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:02.440
but it still, it still makes you

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:03.600
want to play

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:04.920
just a little bit more,

00:38:04.940 --> 00:38:06.080
and then that kind of

00:38:06.080 --> 00:38:07.220
becomes a little bit addictive.

00:38:07.900 --> 00:38:09.700
And also, Yeah, it's not Pac-Man,

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:11.680
mow your lawn levels of satisfying,

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:12.760
but there is

00:38:12.760 --> 00:38:14.760
something satisfying about clearing things

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:15.780
out from the center

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.440
and the short-bursted

00:38:17.440 --> 00:38:18.540
nature of the game.

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:20.700
Yeah, I, I think it did

00:38:20.700 --> 00:38:22.120
give me that satisfaction

00:38:22.120 --> 00:38:24.000
for a little while and make me want

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:25.720
to keep going. But I think that

00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:27.300
the loop being repetitive,

00:38:27.460 --> 00:38:29.200
which is fine, but combined with

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:30.240
the difficulty never

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:31.160
really seeming to get

00:38:31.160 --> 00:38:32.140
that much harder,

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:34.780
didn't, kind of made it to

00:38:34.780 --> 00:38:35.640
about halfway through

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:37.100
the game. I was like, well, that was fun,

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:38.060
and it was fun being

00:38:38.060 --> 00:38:39.460
repetitive for about half the game.

00:38:39.820 --> 00:38:41.300
Maybe it would have gotten better in the

00:38:41.300 --> 00:38:42.580
second half, but for me personally,

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:43.540
I was like, okay, like,

00:38:44.020 --> 00:38:44.840
I don't think I need

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:46.560
to keep going. It's funny because I

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:48.540
would say, past Saturn,

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:49.700
all of a sudden,

00:38:49.720 --> 00:38:50.780
the game does that

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:51.860
thing that most people

00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:52.880
hate, where all of a sudden

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:53.920
it just gets

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.180
a lot more difficult.

00:38:56.520 --> 00:38:57.800
And I don't know if I,

00:38:57.860 --> 00:38:58.940
I wouldn't say it became

00:38:58.940 --> 00:39:00.700
like an incredibly hard game.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:02.120
It was no Ghosts and Goblins

00:39:02.120 --> 00:39:04.240
or anything. It just, for some reason,

00:39:05.500 --> 00:39:06.980
after Saturn felt like

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:08.940
to me, all of a sudden, I was actually dying.

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:10.080
I actually had to be like,

00:39:10.220 --> 00:39:11.920
oh, I'm probably going to run out of lives.

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:13.440
Like, there's just something

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:14.800
about that moment that I remembered.

00:39:14.960 --> 00:39:16.800
And I didn't, I never beat the game

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.340
in its entirety just because of the

00:39:19.340 --> 00:39:20.260
difficulty picking up,

00:39:20.420 --> 00:39:21.560
not because of me

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:22.580
not wanting to beat it.

00:39:22.580 --> 00:39:24.440
Yeah, I think that was more

00:39:24.440 --> 00:39:25.940
of like a difficulty

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:27.600
plateau. Like,

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:29.940
there's only really two or three

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:30.980
difficulty levels

00:39:30.980 --> 00:39:31.920
in this game. Like,

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:33.180
after Saturn is definitely

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:34.300
one of them. And I think

00:39:34.300 --> 00:39:35.220
once you hit Earth,

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:36.540
things get a little

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:37.560
more dicey.

00:39:37.640 --> 00:39:39.000
But it's, it's much more,

00:39:39.620 --> 00:39:40.500
it's a much

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:42.660
gentler gradient

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:43.720
than I'm used to.

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:45.080
And I, I guess usually

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:46.140
I'm complaining about

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:48.120
these games being too hard,

00:39:48.140 --> 00:39:49.120
but I think that

00:39:49.120 --> 00:39:50.100
this hasn't quite hit

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:51.160
that middle level

00:39:51.860 --> 00:39:52.740
of challenge.

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:54.800
Because I was able

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:57.020
to beat the game. I was losing lives,

00:39:57.200 --> 00:39:58.520
but I was able to beat it,

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:00.120
which is weird for a shmup for me.

00:40:00.920 --> 00:40:01.740
Well, that's awesome.

00:40:01.820 --> 00:40:02.660
And that's why you know so much

00:40:02.660 --> 00:40:03.580
about this sun battle.

00:40:05.080 --> 00:40:06.380
Oh, the sun's in that game?

00:40:06.720 --> 00:40:07.620
Oh, the sun says that.

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:34.360
All right.

00:40:34.700 --> 00:40:35.680
Uh, development time.

00:40:35.980 --> 00:40:36.940
I have a small

00:40:36.940 --> 00:40:37.780
little anecdote here

00:40:37.780 --> 00:40:40.140
about Dyrus. It is the second

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:41.660
and last game

00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:43.660
that Yoshiki Akamoto

00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:45.080
designed for Konami.

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:47.240
Now, that might be like,

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:48.920
okay, who's that? Why does that matter?

00:40:49.600 --> 00:40:51.300
Apparently, Akamoto got into

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:52.260
paid disputes

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:53.440
with Konami

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:55.300
over the game and then was fired

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:56.560
after the release

00:40:56.560 --> 00:40:58.620
of this game. So as retaliation,

00:40:58.780 --> 00:40:59.960
he joined rival company

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:00.760
Capcom,

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:01.840
where he went on

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:02.960
to make 1942

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:04.800
and then produced

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:05.840
Street Fighter 2.

00:41:05.840 --> 00:41:07.220
So I think

00:41:07.220 --> 00:41:08.060
Konami

00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:09.060
took a loss there

00:41:09.060 --> 00:41:10.480
over what they

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:11.360
potentially should have

00:41:11.360 --> 00:41:12.440
paid him for this game

00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:13.720
only for him to go on

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:14.920
and make two amazing

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:16.560
franchises for Capcom.

00:41:17.080 --> 00:41:18.400
Wow. So he was like

00:41:19.100 --> 00:41:20.760
Kojima before Kojima.

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:22.240
Well, he wasn't the designer

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:23.340
of Street Fighter 2,

00:41:23.560 --> 00:41:25.480
but, you know, anybody who's involved

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:27.060
in the making

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:27.920
of Street Fighter 2

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:29.140
is kind of a big deal,

00:41:29.240 --> 00:41:30.320
right? Like, you're creating

00:41:30.320 --> 00:41:31.920
an entire genre

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:32.820
and community that's worth

00:41:32.820 --> 00:41:33.720
who knows how many

00:41:33.720 --> 00:41:35.000
millions of dollars

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:36.700
today. For sure.

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:38.740
And then for sequels and spinoffs,

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:39.720
you know, it's interesting.

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:41.640
Many, many ports

00:41:41.640 --> 00:41:43.820
of this game, but no true successor.

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:45.740
There's no Jirus,

00:41:46.680 --> 00:41:47.800
Gyrus, anything.

00:41:48.200 --> 00:41:49.200
You know, I figured they could

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:50.360
change it. Yeah, they could finally

00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:51.360
change it to be

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:52.780
the other ways of spelling it.

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:55.540
There's none of that. And then there's also

00:41:55.540 --> 00:41:56.620
just no, like,

00:41:57.200 --> 00:41:58.040
spiritual successor

00:41:58.040 --> 00:41:59.700
either. So instead,

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:00.720
I just want to talk about a couple

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:02.280
interesting things on the port side.

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:03.580
For the PlayStation

00:42:03.580 --> 00:42:05.780
One, Jirus was actually

00:42:05.780 --> 00:42:07.000
hidden inside of

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:08.500
Contra Legacy of War.

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:10.460
There was like a specific way you could

00:42:10.460 --> 00:42:11.520
unlock Jirus,

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:12.920
and I'm like, wow, that's a weird game

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:14.420
to include in

